#{[#edb/ref 17592186049570 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088461} :activity/id #uuid "2c9e79ce-c186-5278-8e9d-37a961972578" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088874 :tutorial/content "What equation represents a circle of radius $5$ centered at $C$ with coordinates $(1,3)?$\n\nLet's refer to the following figure.\n\n![](images/7a/7adbae4fd1b881931f683d6f4245cd8ee6dfae46fdffcf1b5834448be645d4b5.png)\n\nLet's take a generic point $A$ with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the circumference. Now, we can use the distance formula to write down the length of the radius $CA\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nCA = \\sqrt{(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we know that the radius of the circle is $CA=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(x-1)^2 + (y-3)^2}  = 5.\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides gives us the **equation of the circle** of radius $5$ centered at $C(1,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 + (y-3)^2 = 25.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a circle of radius $r$ centered at point $C$ with coordinates $(a,b),$ the general equation will be\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087462 :question/id #uuid "021d25c6-08db-5f20-a89b-110389d08e29" :question/math-academy-id "e-3224" :question/problem "What is the equation of the circle given below?\n\n![](images/79/79111226781bc9d3ea9c00e217b93b9661d1e70bdfbee688c555dd899cf3d80e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-1,1)$ and the radius of the circle is $r = 3.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and the Radius"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087464 :question/id #uuid "e2d2147e-98df-54df-a271-4c5dff362a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-3226" :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-(-4))^{2} & ={3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a = 2,$ $b = - 4,$ and $r = 3.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(2,-4)$ and its radius is $3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center and Radius of a Circle Given Its Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087465 :question/id #uuid "7e01a50d-62f1-5cc2-875e-bb085ef571c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3230" :question/problem "The line segment $\\overline{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(3,4)$ and $(-1,2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?\n\n![](images/81/81c7d204e134940e4a2cafe465485eb473a81435e3b4f2ca0f291c315d3c37c1.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(3,4)$ and $Y(-1,2),$ we find that the length the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(2-4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{XY}{2} = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, we can find the center using the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2}, \\dfrac {y_1+y_2}{2}\\right) = \\left( \\dfrac{3+(-1)}{2}, \\dfrac {4+2}{2}\\right)=(1,3)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and center $(a,b)=(1,3),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Endpoints of a Diameter"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087585 :question/id #uuid "16faff9a-9599-5b8c-85cd-132f3e3f4b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6150" :question/problem "The circle below is centered at $C(2, 0)$ and the point $X(-1, 4)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle.\n\n![](images/ec/ec8cd56c5a957321de6867c329d53839a924f66714de9941ebb088081680cfbf.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(2-(-1))}^{2}+{(0-4)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and a Point on the Circle"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088464}}] :activity/title "Equations of Circles" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087914}] [#edb/ref 17592186049592 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167625} :activity/id #uuid "646099ed-0bc0-5178-b252-fcbaf49d8855" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168069 :tutorial/content "An **ordinary differential equation** is an equation that involves a function with one variable (usually denoted as $y$), its derivatives, and an independent variable (usually denoted as $x$).\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}-3xy^2 = y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nis an ordinary differential equation (ODE). \n\n**Watch out!** Here, the function $y=y(x)$ actually depends on the independent variable $x.$\n\nThe **order of an ordinary differential equation** is the order of the highest derivative in the equation. \n\nIn our example above, the highest derivative is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2},\n$$\n\nwhich has order $2.$ Therefore, the order of the ODE is $2.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166651 :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166652 :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166653 :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168084 :tutorial/content "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be **autonomous** if it can be written in the form \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),\n$$\n\nor equivalently, \n\n$$\ny'=f(y).\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -5y^2\n$$\n\nis autonomous, while the equation \n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{y+2}{x^2}\n$$\n\nis *not* autonomous since the expression for $y'$ on the right-hand side contains the independent variable $x$ explicitly, outside the function $y=y(x).$" :tutorial/title "Autonomous Ordinary Differential Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168085 :tutorial/content "A first-order **initial value problem** (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}, \\qquad {\\color{red}y(e)=-2}\n$$\n\nis a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}\n$$\n\ntogether with the initial condition \n\n$$\n{\\color{red} y(e)=-2}.\n$$" :tutorial/title "First-Order Initial Value Problems"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167652}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Differential Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166999}] [#edb/ref 17592186049633 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180451} :activity/id #uuid "51b352cb-6801-5faf-93b4-42be670a13a1" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179571 :question/id #uuid "4088c5c5-6f69-54f3-9029-3ba52f5b00d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9435" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2e4e808d8264685f9b88c5b0052870174d8be3ed2a7195149d45552118186a49.png)\n\nBy setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9 , 3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180457}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180702 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the line integral with respect to arc length\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm ds,\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y),$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y^2 = -2x$ traversed between the points $(-8,-4)$ and the origin, as shown below. \n\n![](images/21/21274ce432ea1a47192122bd972878f80d29afd637b6ba4fb37fe3d75b74f959.png)\n\nThe first thing is to find a parametrization for $C.$ One option is to write the equation of the curve in the form $y = y(x).$ However, as this involves dealing with square roots, it can get messy. \n\nA more elegant way is to write the equation of $C$ in the form $x = x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny^2 = -2x\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = -\\dfrac12 y^2\n$$\n\nSince $x$ is now an explicit function of the independent variable $y,$ we can set $y=t,$ and this gives\n\n$$\nx(t) = -\\dfrac12 t^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we now have the following parametrization for $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle-\\dfrac12t^2,\\, t\\right\\rangle,\\quad -4 \\leq t \\leq 0,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere the bounds for $t$ are the $y$-values at the path's endpoints. We can now write an expression for our line integral in terms of $t$ in the usual way." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180443}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179580 :question/id #uuid "f025deb6-7c71-5d15-9966-52f7d5eeab80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9463" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0cebdd804ff244d037e14df6ec33efb86b522644f7b4addc688e27fd6a57e95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2 , 2\\sqrt{2}\\right),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Function Over Curve Expressed as a Function of Y"}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Paths Expressed as Functions of Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179844}] [#edb/ref 17592186049639 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199269} :activity/id #uuid "9c0cd1dd-d41d-511e-aa18-44cf1df53bbc" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199461 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\\mathbf T: (u,v,w) \\longrightarrow \\left(x(u,v,w), \\, y(u,v,w), \\, z(u,v,w) \\right)$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^3,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = R,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d7875753545cba8babd08fccaf55236c6b32bfc56f43f496343174acc198f1d9.png)\n\nTo compute the integral of a function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R,$ we use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right| \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for double integrals." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198039 :question/id #uuid "d123b7fd-5a13-50c0-aa8c-dc196f4b1bcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12406" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in Cartesian $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:1 \\leq xy \\leq  2, \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z\\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,  \\qquad\nv=y, \\qquad\nw=2z,\n$$\n\n \n\nwrite down the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint\\limits_R 3xy^2+6yz \\, \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c03fec53fda9106ca287f15afd0d07b1adddf00675b2bdbec8a658fbdf06aa.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,  \\qquad\nv=y, \\qquad\nw=2z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/64/643fb7a9bbc46ac2a6fe8c6d89f4fcb7f2db206c7af11f7cde9e1a34da8ac201.png)\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(y-0) \\\\  & =2y \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3x{y}^{2}+6yz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(3uv+3vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}3v(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables in a Triple Integral"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198040 :question/id #uuid "3fccb151-d67f-525b-8804-62589913084a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12407" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint \\limits_R (x+y^2)^{15}z \\: \\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:  0 \\leq x+y^2 \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y+z \\leq 4, \\: 0\\leq z\\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/eb/eb8f40c6ffabf36ae5a305c7c7b71683b914b537f64766a20aac09d3666fac81.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/4b/4bcd5430181c2cb411dfe984b04399f2f80ab9d1747d5cf82596d105419b2b4f.png)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{15}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}\\cdot \\frac{w}{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{15}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{15}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{16}}{16}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=4}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Using a Change of Variables"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199313}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198399 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198041 :question/id #uuid "e00a8e15-e738-5fee-80f7-405844fa1422" :question/math-academy-id "e-12408" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\:-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8 \\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/54/54035a0d0a2dd88eb0b4b89bb1162df2cf9b450a4ea4d5b5020163053880016f.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\qquad\n-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\qquad\n1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y, \\qquad\nv = y-2x, \\qquad\nw = z^3.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/4b/4bcd5430181c2cb411dfe984b04399f2f80ab9d1747d5cf82596d105419b2b4f.png)\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-2\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac 19 w^{-2/3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [v]_{-2}^{2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-(-2))\\cdot 3({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Region Using a Change of Variables"}}] :activity/title "Computing Triple Integrals Using a Change of Variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198490}]}
